#
# prima-materia — Chan
#
# Opened from the structure-driven entrance of SPEC §14: registered at
# pm:corpusNamed in traditions/registry-buddhist.ttl with no node. What was
# opened is the first of the three works the registration names, the Platform
# Sūtra, in the recension of Zongbao of 1291 as the Taishō prints it, read
# complete from the first chapter to the tenth in the Chinese; the Dunhuang
# text, the earliest witness, was read at every passage entered here to see
# whether the passage is in it. The two other works stay unopened and are
# declared below.
#
# Order of the file: the works; the tradition with the three decisions; the
# practices and what they are said to bring about; the powers the text
# ascribes to Huineng; the cautions, nearly all against one way of sitting;
# what the text says a failed practice looks like; the one act of assay and
# the one sign; and the dispute over whether sitting is needed at all.
#
# Citation. Every node cites the Chinese by chapter of the Zongbao recension and
# by the line locus of the Taishō edition, volume 48, number 2008, which is the
# locus every edition of the canon carries; and two English translations by
# two translators, Thomas Cleary's and that of the Buddhist Text Translation
# Society, by page. The second prints the sutra in sections with a long
# commentary between them, and only the sections marked as sutra were read as
# translation.
#

#
# The works
#

pmw:LiuzuTanjing a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Liuzu dashi fabao tanjing, the Platform Sūtra of the Sixth Patriarch"@en, "Liuzu dashi fabao tanjing, das Plattform-Sūtra des Sechsten Patriarchen"@de ;
    skos:note "Read complete on 2026-10-14 in the Chinese of the Zongbao recension, Liuzu dashi fabao tanjing, Taishō vol. 48 no. 2008, pp. 345b-365a, from the CBETA TEI file T48n2008, the ten chapters from the account of Huineng's life to the entrustment, and the appendices read for the record of the head. The Dunhuang text, Nanzong dunjiao zuishang dasheng mohe banruo boluomi jing, Taishō vol. 48 no. 2007, from the CBETA file T48n2007, was read at every passage entered."@en ;
    skos:note "Two translations, by two translators, both of the Zongbao recension: Thomas Cleary, The Sutra of Hui-neng, Grand Master of Zen (Boston: Shambhala 1998), the title page in the full text; and the Buddhist Text Translation Society, The Sixth Patriarch's Dharma Jewel Platform Sutra, with the commentary of Hsuan Hua, third edition (Burlingame: Buddhist Text Translation Society 2001), the title page in the full text. Access: archive.org, the-sutra-of-hui-neng-grand-master-of-zen-with_commentry_on_the_diamond_sutra and the-sixth-patriarchs-dharma-jewel-platform-sutra, both with a clean English text layer. Philip Yampolsky's translation of the Dunhuang text, 1967, lies at archive.org only under loan restriction and was not read."@en ;
    skos:note "The recension is the first thing a reader of this file has to know, because it decides what may be cited as early. The Dunhuang text is a manuscript of the late eighth or ninth century and is a little over half the length of the Zongbao text. Much of what the later recension carries is not in it at all: the cases of Zhihuang's twenty years of sitting, of the assassin whose sword did no harm, of the spring struck from the ground, of the samadhi of sport and of the prophecy of the head, the phrase that stopping the mind and contemplating stillness is a sickness and not Chan, and the nine years Zhicheng studied without awakening. Where the Dunhuang text has a case, its numbers can differ: Fada there recites the Lotus Sūtra for seven years, where the Zongbao text has three thousand recitations. Each node says whether its passage is in the Dunhuang text. A node whose passage is only in the Zongbao text attests the recension of 1291 and the layer between, and not the eighth century."@en .

pmw:LinjiLu a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Linji lu, the Record of Linji"@en, "Linji lu, die Aufzeichnungen des Linji"@de ;
    skos:note "Named by the registration and not opened in this run. Reachable: the Chinese stands in the CBETA TEI file T47n1985 (Zhenzhou Linji Huizhao chanshi yulu, Taishō vol. 47 no. 1985), checked on 2026-10-14 to answer with a full file. No translation was looked for."@en .

pmw:BiyanLu a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Biyan lu, the Blue Cliff Record"@en, "Biyan lu, die Niederschrift von der Smaragdenen Felswand"@de ;
    skos:note "Named by the registration and not opened in this run. Reachable: the Chinese stands in the CBETA TEI file T48n2003 (Foguo Yuanwu chanshi Biyan lu, Taishō vol. 48 no. 2003), checked on 2026-10-14 to answer with a full file. No translation was looked for."@en .

#
# The tradition
#

pmt:Chan a pm:Tradition ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiuzuTanjing ;
    rdfs:label "Chan"@en, "Chan"@de ;
    skos:definition "The Chinese transmission holding that awakening is sudden and that the mind is not to be polished but recognised."@en ;
    skos:note "Contact route, the first of the three decisions SPEC §14 requires, kept at documented, and the route is in the text. The Platform Sūtra quotes the Diamond, Vimalakīrti, Nirvāṇa, Lotus and Laṅkāvatāra sūtras and the Brahmajāla precepts by name and argues from them; it is a Chinese reading of translated Indian scripture. Its warnings against quiet sitting answer a Chinese meditation teaching it names, the East Mountain school of Hongren and Shenxiu. Any agreement between this file and the Indian or Tiantai files of this holding is reception along that route and counts once."@en ;
    skos:note "Recension and edition, the second decision. The Zongbao recension of 1291 is read as the text and the Dunhuang text as the control, because the Zongbao text is the one the registration's later works stand on and the Dunhuang text is the one that dates a passage. The difference is large and is recorded at the work and at each node."@en ;
    skos:note "The third decision, the number of citable places: the ten chapters, of which every chapter carries at least one node below."@en ;
    skos:note "What the reading found that a reader would not expect from the registration's one-line definition. The text names many capacities — seeing the nature and being a buddha in that moment, the extinction of the sins of eons by a verse, the sight of Amitābha in a finger-snap, a samadhi of sport in the spiritual powers — and almost none of them carries a condition under which it would be found absent, beyond the hearer's own failure to awaken. What the text does carry, in seven places and in both recensions, is a warning against one way of sitting, and once a consequence that is no figure of speech: those who are taught to sit watching the mind and contemplating purity, without moving, grasp it and become deranged. And it reports, by name and with a count of years or recitations, six persons who practised long under other methods and did not awaken. The numbers are almost all in the later recension."@en ;
    skos:note "Signs at the death of the master are reported at the end of the tenth chapter — a strange fragrance filling the room, a white rainbow touching the earth, the trees turning white, the birds and beasts crying, incense smoke pointing the way home, a white light from the stupa for three days. They are signs of the master's attainment told by his community and not signs a practitioner is told to look for, and they are not entered as nodes."@en ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Platform Sūtra; Linji lu; Biyan lu" ;
    pm:corpusWork pmw:LiuzuTanjing, pmw:LinjiLu, pmw:BiyanLu ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    dcterms:source "Liuzu dashi fabao tanjing, ch. 1 Xingyou, T48 no. 2008, 348a10" ;
    dcterms:source "Liuzu dashi fabao tanjing, ch. 4 Dinghui, T48 no. 2008, 352c13-353a05" .

#
# The practices and what they are said to bring about
#

pmc:ChanJianxingChengfo a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiuzuTanjing ;
    rdfs:label "Seeing the nature and becoming a buddha, in a single moment of awakening"@en, "Die eigene Natur sehen und Buddha werden, in einem einzigen Augenblick des Erwachens"@de ;
    skos:definition "The capacity the whole text turns on: to see one's own nature, jianxing, is directly to become a buddha, zhi liao cheng fo. Hongren tells Huineng that one who recognises his own original mind and sees his own original nature is called a hero, a teacher of gods and men, a buddha; the second chapter says that the moment before, deluded, is an ordinary man, the moment after, awakened, is a buddha; that with one awakening one reaches the stage of a buddha; and that the deluded man is a buddha the moment he awakens."@en ;
    skos:note "The capacity is stated as sudden and as total, and it carries one claim about time that could be held against it: it happens at the words, yanxia, and not after deliberation. That is the criterion entered at pmc:ChanJiekanYan, and the text's own reports of awakening keep to it — Huineng at the line of the Diamond Sūtra, Huiming at the question about his original face, Fada, Zhicheng, Zhihuang each at a sentence."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Chan ;
    dcterms:source "Liuzu dashi fabao tanjing, ch. 1 Xingyou and ch. 2 Banruo, T48 no. 2008, 349a19-a22, 350b28, 351a09 and 351a25" ;
    dcterms:source "The Sutra of Hui-neng, Grand Master of Zen, trans. Thomas Cleary (Boston: Shambhala 1998), chs. 1-2, pp. 10-11 and 17-21" ;
    dcterms:source "The Sixth Patriarch's Dharma Jewel Platform Sutra, trans. Buddhist Text Translation Society, 3rd ed. (Burlingame 2001), chs. 1-2" .

pmp:ChanChiJingangJing a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiuzuTanjing ;
    rdfs:label "Holding and reciting the Diamond Sūtra"@en, "Das Diamant-Sūtra halten und rezitieren"@de ;
    skos:definition "The practice the text gives twice by name. In the first chapter a stranger tells Huineng that Hongren always urges monks and laymen only to hold the Diamond Sūtra; in the second Huineng says that whoever would enter the deep realm of the dharma and the samadhi of prajñā must cultivate the practice of prajñā and hold and recite the Diamond Prajñā Sūtra."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Chan ;
    dcterms:source "Liuzu dashi fabao tanjing, ch. 1 Xingyou and ch. 2 Banruo, T48 no. 2008, 348a10 and 350c10" ;
    dcterms:source "The Sutra of Hui-neng, Grand Master of Zen, trans. Thomas Cleary (Boston: Shambhala 1998), chs. 1-2, pp. 6 and 19" ;
    dcterms:source "The Sixth Patriarch's Dharma Jewel Platform Sutra, trans. Buddhist Text Translation Society, 3rd ed. (Burlingame 2001), chs. 1-2, pp. 56 and 131" .

pmc:ChanChiJingJianxing a pm:Yielding ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiuzuTanjing ;
    rdfs:label "Holding the Diamond Sūtra brings one to see the nature"@en, "Das Halten des Diamant-Sūtra führt dazu, die Natur zu sehen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The claim, made twice: if one only holds the Diamond Sūtra one will oneself see the nature and directly become a buddha, as Hongren is reported to say; and whoever holds and recites the Diamond Prajñā Sūtra will see the nature, the merit of this sūtra being without measure, as Huineng says."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChanChiJingangJing ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChanJianxingChengfo ;
    skos:note "Both places are in the Dunhuang text. The same chapter that promises the result also says what recitation without the mind is worth, entered at pmc:ChanKounianXinbuxing: a man who recites prajñā all day without knowing the prajñā of his own nature is like one who talks about food and is not fed. The claim and its limit stand in the same chapter and were never reconciled in it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Chan ;
    dcterms:source "Liuzu dashi fabao tanjing, ch. 1 Xingyou and ch. 2 Banruo, T48 no. 2008, 348a10 and 350c10" ;
    dcterms:source "Nanzong dunjiao zuishang dasheng mohe banruo boluomi jing, Dunhuang text, T48 no. 2007, 337a21-a26" ;
    dcterms:source "The Sutra of Hui-neng, Grand Master of Zen, trans. Thomas Cleary (Boston: Shambhala 1998), chs. 1-2, pp. 6 and 19" ;
    dcterms:source "The Sixth Patriarch's Dharma Jewel Platform Sutra, trans. Buddhist Text Translation Society, 3rd ed. (Burlingame 2001), chs. 1-2, pp. 56 and 131" .

pmp:ChanWuxiangChanhui a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiuzuTanjing ;
    rdfs:label "The formless repentance and the recitation of the formless verse"@en, "Die formlose Reue und die Rezitation des formlosen Verses"@de ;
    skos:definition "The rite of the sixth chapter. The assembly kneels; the five incenses of the self-nature are transmitted — precept, concentration, wisdom, liberation and the knowledge of liberation, each an inner state; then the formless repentance is spoken after the master three times, that in the past, the present and the coming thought one be not stained by folly, by pride and deceit, by envy, and that all such past evil karma be repented and extinguished at once and never arise again; then the four great vows, the threefold refuge in awakening, the right and the pure, and the three bodies of the buddha in one's own body. At the end Huineng gives a formless verse to be memorised and recited."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Chan ;
    dcterms:source "Liuzu dashi fabao tanjing, ch. 6 Chanhui, T48 no. 2008, 353b29-354c29" ;
    dcterms:source "The Sutra of Hui-neng, Grand Master of Zen, trans. Thomas Cleary (Boston: Shambhala 1998), ch. 6, pp. 36-44" ;
    dcterms:source "The Sixth Patriarch's Dharma Jewel Platform Sutra, trans. Buddhist Text Translation Society, 3rd ed. (Burlingame 2001), ch. 6, pp. 221-259" .

pmc:ChanZuiMie a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiuzuTanjing ;
    rdfs:label "The sins of the three times and of accumulated eons extinguished at once"@en, "Die Sünden der drei Zeiten und angesammelter Äonen auf einmal getilgt"@de ;
    skos:definition "The capacity claimed for the rite: the formless repentance extinguishes the sins of the three times and makes the three karmas pure; and of the formless verse, that if one can recite and hold it, at the words the delusions and sins of accumulated eons are extinguished at once."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Chan ;
    dcterms:source "Liuzu dashi fabao tanjing, ch. 6 Chanhui, T48 no. 2008, 353c17 and 354c25" ;
    dcterms:source "The Sutra of Hui-neng, Grand Master of Zen, trans. Thomas Cleary (Boston: Shambhala 1998), ch. 6, pp. 38 and 42-43" ;
    dcterms:source "The Sixth Patriarch's Dharma Jewel Platform Sutra, trans. Buddhist Text Translation Society, 3rd ed. (Burlingame 2001), ch. 6, pp. 221-222" .

pmc:ChanChanhuiMieZui a pm:Yielding ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiuzuTanjing ;
    rdfs:label "The formless repentance and its verse extinguish the sins of eons"@en, "Die formlose Reue und ihr Vers tilgen die Sünden von Äonen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The claim of effect of the sixth chapter: the repentance, spoken in the three forms after the master, extinguishes the sins of the three times; the verse, recited, extinguishes at the words the sins of accumulated eons."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChanWuxiangChanhui ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChanZuiMie ;
    skos:note "The text attaches the condition under which the rite fails in the same chapter, and it is a condition on the hearer: ordinary people repent only of their past faults and do not vow against future ones, and so the past faults are not extinguished and new ones arise. And at the end: whoever does not awaken at these words is a thousand li away though face to face with the master. The verse with its claim of extinction is in the Dunhuang text."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Chan ;
    dcterms:source "Liuzu dashi fabao tanjing, ch. 6 Chanhui, T48 no. 2008, 353c17-354a08 and 354c25-355a09" ;
    dcterms:source "Nanzong dunjiao zuishang dasheng mohe banruo boluomi jing, Dunhuang text, T48 no. 2007, 341a05-a09" ;
    dcterms:source "The Sutra of Hui-neng, Grand Master of Zen, trans. Thomas Cleary (Boston: Shambhala 1998), ch. 6, pp. 38-43" ;
    dcterms:source "The Sixth Patriarch's Dharma Jewel Platform Sutra, trans. Buddhist Text Translation Society, 3rd ed. (Burlingame 2001), ch. 6, pp. 221-259" .

pmp:ChanXinjingXingShan a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiuzuTanjing ;
    rdfs:label "Purifying one's own mind and practising the ten good deeds in place of the vow of rebirth"@en, "Den eigenen Geist reinigen und die zehn guten Taten üben statt des Gelübdes der Wiedergeburt"@de ;
    skos:definition "The practice the third chapter puts in place of the recitation of the Buddha's name with the vow to be reborn in the western land: first remove the ten evils, then the eight wrongs; in every thought see the nature and always act evenly and straightly; practise the ten good deeds. The chapter adds that this can be done at home and does not depend on the monastery, and gives the formless verse of conduct — even mind, straight action, gratitude, duty, deference, patience — to be followed."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Chan ;
    dcterms:source "Liuzu dashi fabao tanjing, ch. 3 Yiwen, T48 no. 2008, 352a08-c13" ;
    dcterms:source "The Sutra of Hui-neng, Grand Master of Zen, trans. Thomas Cleary (Boston: Shambhala 1998), ch. 3, pp. 26-30" ;
    dcterms:source "The Sixth Patriarch's Dharma Jewel Platform Sutra, trans. Buddhist Text Translation Society, 3rd ed. (Burlingame 2001), ch. 3, pp. 186-196" .

pmc:ChanJianMituo a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiuzuTanjing ;
    rdfs:label "Seeing Amitābha in a finger-snap, the western land before one's eyes"@en, "Amitābha in einem Fingerschnippen sehen, das Westland vor Augen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The capacity claimed in the third chapter: to arrive as in a finger-snap and see Amitābha; for one who awakens to the sudden teaching of no-birth, to see the West in an instant; Huineng offers to move the West before the assembly's eyes, and the assembly, hearing him, clearly sees the nature."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Chan ;
    dcterms:source "Liuzu dashi fabao tanjing, ch. 3 Yiwen, T48 no. 2008, 352a26-b13" ;
    dcterms:source "The Sutra of Hui-neng, Grand Master of Zen, trans. Thomas Cleary (Boston: Shambhala 1998), ch. 3, pp. 27-28" ;
    dcterms:source "The Sixth Patriarch's Dharma Jewel Platform Sutra, trans. Buddhist Text Translation Society, 3rd ed. (Burlingame 2001), ch. 3, pp. 187-188" .

pmc:ChanXinjingJianMituo a pm:Yielding ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiuzuTanjing ;
    rdfs:label "A purified mind and the ten good deeds bring the sight of Amitābha in a finger-snap"@en, "Gereinigter Geist und zehn gute Taten bringen den Anblick Amitābhas in einem Fingerschnippen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The claim of the third chapter: having removed the ten evils and the eight wrongs, seeing the nature in every thought and always acting evenly, one arrives as in a finger-snap and sees Amitābha; if the practitioner only does the ten good deeds, what need to vow to be reborn there."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChanXinjingXingShan ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChanJianMituo ;
    skos:note "The failing case is stated in the same place, and it is the only condition in the chapter that a third party could in principle check: with a mind of evil intent, even reciting the Buddha's name, one hardly arrives. The chapter turns the Pure Land claim into a claim about the practitioner's conduct and lets the conduct, and not the recitation, decide."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Chan ;
    dcterms:source "Liuzu dashi fabao tanjing, ch. 3 Yiwen, T48 no. 2008, 352a26-b13" ;
    dcterms:source "Nanzong dunjiao zuishang dasheng mohe banruo boluomi jing, Dunhuang text, T48 no. 2007, 341b06-b14" ;
    dcterms:source "The Sutra of Hui-neng, Grand Master of Zen, trans. Thomas Cleary (Boston: Shambhala 1998), ch. 3, pp. 27-28" ;
    dcterms:source "The Sixth Patriarch's Dharma Jewel Platform Sutra, trans. Buddhist Text Translation Society, 3rd ed. (Burlingame 2001), ch. 3, pp. 187-188" .

pmp:ChanYixingSanmei a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiuzuTanjing ;
    rdfs:label "The samadhi of one practice and the samadhi of one mark"@en, "Das Samādhi der einen Übung und das Samādhi des einen Merkmals"@de ;
    skos:definition "The two samadhis as the text defines them. The samadhi of one practice is to act always with one straight mind in every place, walking, standing, sitting and lying; the straight mind is the place of enlightenment and the pure land. The samadhi of one mark, added in the tenth chapter, is in every place not to dwell in marks, to have no love or hate toward them, nothing to take or reject, not to think of gain or loss, at ease, quiet, empty and plain."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Chan ;
    dcterms:source "Liuzu dashi fabao tanjing, ch. 4 Dinghui and ch. 10 Fuzhu, T48 no. 2008, 352c25-353a01 and 361a28-b03" ;
    dcterms:source "The Sutra of Hui-neng, Grand Master of Zen, trans. Thomas Cleary (Boston: Shambhala 1998), chs. 4 and 10, pp. 31 and 75" ;
    dcterms:source "The Sixth Patriarch's Dharma Jewel Platform Sutra, trans. Buddhist Text Translation Society, 3rd ed. (Burlingame 2001), ch. 10, pp. 395-397" .

pmc:ChanZhongzhi a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiuzuTanjing ;
    rdfs:label "All-knowledge and the fruit of bodhi"@en, "Allwissen und die Frucht der Erleuchtung"@de ;
    skos:definition "The capacity the tenth chapter names: the knowledge of all modes, zhongzhi, and bodhi as the fruit that ripens of itself, like a seed in ground that has had rain."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Chan ;
    dcterms:source "Liuzu dashi fabao tanjing, ch. 10 Fuzhu, T48 no. 2008, 361a27-b09" ;
    dcterms:source "The Sutra of Hui-neng, Grand Master of Zen, trans. Thomas Cleary (Boston: Shambhala 1998), ch. 10, p. 75" ;
    dcterms:source "The Sixth Patriarch's Dharma Jewel Platform Sutra, trans. Buddhist Text Translation Society, 3rd ed. (Burlingame 2001), ch. 10, p. 395" .

pmc:ChanSanmeiZhongzhi a pm:Yielding ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiuzuTanjing ;
    rdfs:label "The two samadhis bring all-knowledge, and whoever follows the master will surely attain bodhi"@en, "Die beiden Samādhis bringen Allwissen, und wer dem Meister folgt, erlangt sicher die Erleuchtung"@de ;
    skos:definition "The claim of the tenth chapter: whoever wishes to accomplish the knowledge of all modes must attain the samadhi of one mark and the samadhi of one practice; one who has both is like ground with seeds in it that holds, nourishes and ripens its fruit; whoever receives my meaning will certainly attain bodhi, whoever follows my practice will surely realise the wondrous fruit."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChanYixingSanmei ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChanZhongzhi ;
    skos:note "Only in the Zongbao text; the Dunhuang text has the samadhi of one practice in its fourth section and nothing of the samadhi of one mark or of all-knowledge. The sentence ends with the word certainly, jue, and with no term, so the promise cannot fall due."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Chan ;
    dcterms:source "Liuzu dashi fabao tanjing, ch. 10 Fuzhu, T48 no. 2008, 361a27-b09" ;
    dcterms:source "The Sutra of Hui-neng, Grand Master of Zen, trans. Thomas Cleary (Boston: Shambhala 1998), ch. 10, p. 75" ;
    dcterms:source "The Sixth Patriarch's Dharma Jewel Platform Sutra, trans. Buddhist Text Translation Society, 3rd ed. (Burlingame 2001), ch. 10, pp. 395-397" .

pmp:ChanBusiShanE a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiuzuTanjing ;
    rdfs:label "Thinking neither of good nor of evil"@en, "Weder an Gutes noch an Böses denken"@de ;
    skos:definition "The instruction given twice in the Zongbao text. To Huiming on the mountain pass: since you have come for the dharma, set aside all conditions and do not give rise to a single thought; then, not thinking of good, not thinking of evil, at just this moment, what is your original face? To the envoy Xue Jian in the ninth chapter: if you wish to know the essentials of the mind, simply do not think of anything good or evil at all."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Chan ;
    dcterms:source "Liuzu dashi fabao tanjing, ch. 1 Xingyou and ch. 9 Xuanzhao, T48 no. 2008, 349b23-b29 and 360a13-a15" ;
    dcterms:source "The Sutra of Hui-neng, Grand Master of Zen, trans. Thomas Cleary (Boston: Shambhala 1998), chs. 1 and 9, pp. 12-13 and 69" ;
    dcterms:source "The Sixth Patriarch's Dharma Jewel Platform Sutra, trans. Buddhist Text Translation Society, 3rd ed. (Burlingame 2001), chs. 1 and 9, pp. 106 and 376" .

#ANKER-CHAN-PUSH
